---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocTable from "../../components/docs/DocTable/DocTable.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout/Callout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Coding agents - Arris Docs"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Docs", href: "/" }, { label: "AI" }, { label: "Coding agents" }]}
  heading="Coding agents"
  lede="Ask Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or GitHub Copilot data questions. They work through the arris CLI: the agent reads your schema, writes the query, and puts it in an Arris console tab where you see the same result."
  tocItems={[
    { label: "Install", href: "#install" },
    { label: "How it works", href: "#how" },
    { label: "Connect a database", href: "#connect" },
    { label: "Ask a question", href: "#query" },
    { label: "Changing data", href: "#writes" },
    { label: "Point at what you see", href: "#context" },
    { label: "Canvases", href: "#canvas" },
    { label: "dbt and SQLMesh", href: "#analytics" },
    { label: "Security", href: "#security" },
    { label: "CLI reference", href: "#cli" },
  ]}
  currentPath="/ai/coding-agents"
  prev={{ label: "SQLMesh", href: "/analytics-engineering/sqlmesh", section: "Analytics engineering" }}
  next={{ label: "Keyboard shortcuts", href: "/reference/shortcuts", section: "Reference" }}
>
  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>

  <p>Run this once in a terminal:</p>

  <CodeBlock lang="sh">
    npx arris
  </CodeBlock>

  <p>
    It installs the <code>arris</code> binary to <code>~/.arris/bin/arris</code> and the
    <code>arris</code> skill to two folders that cover all four agents:
  </p>

  <DocTable>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Agent</th>
        <th>Reads the skill from</th>
        <th>Invoke it with</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Claude Code</td>
        <td><code>~/.claude/skills/arris</code></td>
        <td><code>/arris</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Codex</td>
        <td><code>~/.agents/skills/arris</code></td>
        <td><code>$arris</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Cursor</td>
        <td>both of the above</td>
        <td><code>/arris</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">GitHub Copilot</td>
        <td><code>~/.agents/skills/arris</code> (VS Code also reads <code>~/.claude/skills</code>)</td>
        <td><code>/arris</code> in VS Code chat</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocTable>

  <p>
    Restart your agent after installing. Run <code>npx arris</code> again to upgrade; the previous
    copy is moved to <code>~/.arris/skill-backups</code>. <code>npx arris --project</code> installs the
    skill into the current project's <code>.claude/skills</code> and <code>.agents/skills</code>
    instead. It runs on macOS and Linux.
  </p>

  <Callout type="note" title="The skill may be listed twice">
    Cursor and Copilot in VS Code load skills from both folders, so they can show two
    <code>arris</code> entries. They are the same skill.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>

  <p>
    The agent never talks to your databases directly. It runs <code>arris</code> commands, which
    print JSON. The first command starts a small Arris server on your machine (bound to
    <code>127.0.0.1</code> and protected by a login token). The same server serves the Arris UI in
    your browser, and the desktop app follows it too, so whatever the agent does shows up in the Arris
    window you have open.
  </p>

  <p>Type <code>/arris</code> (<code>$arris</code> in Codex) with an optional first word:</p>

  <DocTable>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Command</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>/arris</code></td>
        <td>Opens the project's Arris UI in the browser.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>/arris init-connection</code></td>
        <td>Sets up a new connection.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>/arris &lt;question&gt;</code></td>
        <td>Answers a data question with a query. <code>/arris query &lt;question&gt;</code> is the same.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>/arris dbt</code>, <code>/arris sqlmesh</code></td>
        <td>Works with the project's models, lineage, and commands.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocTable>

  <p>
    You can also just ask in plain words, for example <em>"how many orders shipped last week?"</em>;
    the agent picks up the skill on its own.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connect">Connect a database</h2>

  <p>
    <code>/arris init-connection</code> opens the connection form in your browser. You fill it in
    there, including the password; the agent only learns the connection's name. If you give it a
    connection URI, the form opens with host, port, user, database, and options filled in, and you
    type the password yourself. To change a saved connection, ask the agent to edit it: the same form
    opens with the saved values.
  </p>

  <Callout type="note" title="Keychain prompt">
    After you save, macOS may ask whether <code>arris</code> can use the Keychain. Click
    <strong>Always Allow</strong>; the command waits until you do.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="query">Ask a question</h2>

  <p>
    The agent lists your connections, reads the schema, and writes the query. Every query from one
    agent session goes to that session's console tab for the connection, under a comment saying what
    it answers. Ask for a separate tab if you want one. The tab is named after the agent:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claude Code</strong> and <strong>Codex</strong>: the agent plus a short session id, such as <code>Claude 1a2b3c4d</code> or <code>Codex 1a2b3c4d</code>, so two sessions never share a tab.</li>
    <li><strong>Cursor</strong>: <code>Cursor</code>. Cursor does not expose a chat id, so all its chats share one tab per connection.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub Copilot</strong>, and anything else running the CLI: <code>Agent</code>. Copilot does not tell the commands it runs which chat they come from, so its chats share one tab per connection.</li>
  </ul>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reads run right away.</strong> The tab shows the result, and the agent answers in a short note instead of repeating the rows.</li>
    <li><strong>One Arris tab is enough.</strong> If no Arris window is open, the first query opens one; later queries reuse it.</li>
    <li><strong>Only the window you are looking at takes a query.</strong> A tab in the background keeps it queued and shows it as soon as you switch to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Large results stay in Arris.</strong> The agent reads further pages of an earlier run from its saved result instead of running the query again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="writes">Changing data</h2>

  <p>
    The agent does not run <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, DDL, or MongoDB
    writes unless you asked for that change:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>You asked for it</strong> (for example, <em>"delete the 3 test orders"</em>): it runs, the tab shows the result, and the agent tells you what changed.</li>
    <li><strong>The request is unclear, or the statement would change much more than you described</strong> (such as a <code>DELETE</code> with no <code>WHERE</code>): the agent shows the SQL and asks first.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything else</strong> lands in the console without running. Press <strong>Run</strong> in Arris if you want it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Arris decides what counts as a read per database. A statement it cannot prove read-only is
    treated as a write.
  </p>

  <h2 id="context">Point at what you see</h2>

  <p>
    The agent can read what the Arris window shows: the active tab, its text, and your selection. So you
    can say <em>"explain this query"</em> or <em>"why is the selected part slow?"</em> without pasting
    anything. It can also list the open tabs and close them, for example <em>"close every tab but
    this one"</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="canvas">Canvases</h2>

  <p>
    Ask for a dashboard, or to put a result on a <a href="/canvas">canvas</a>. The agent runs the query,
    then adds it to a new or existing board, with a chart when you asked for one. The open Arris tab
    switches to the board, which runs its read queries and draws the chart. It can also redraw a
    chart with another type or axes, remove an object, or delete a whole canvas when you ask.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analytics">dbt and SQLMesh</h2>

  <p>
    In a <a href="/analytics-engineering/dbt">dbt</a> or <a href="/analytics-engineering/sqlmesh">SQLMesh</a>
    project, the agent reads models, their SQL and columns, and model or column lineage. It runs tool
    commands as <code>arris dbt cli -- &lt;args&gt;</code> (or <code>arris sqlmesh cli</code>), so each
    run is logged and shows up in Arris. It asks before commands that write to the warehouse unless
    you asked for them. To query a model's table without a connection yet, it creates one from the
    dbt profile target or SQLMesh gateway through the same browser form.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">Security</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Passwords and keys are typed only into the browser form and kept in the system keychain. They never appear in the agent's output, in commands, or in what the CLI prints.</li>
    <li>Never paste a password into the chat; the skill tells the agent never to ask for one.</li>
    <li>The server listens only on <code>127.0.0.1</code> and needs its login token. Some commands print a URL that contains the token: keep it on your machine.</li>
    <li>The agent sees the rows its own queries return, as with any tool it runs. Use <code>--limit</code> or aggregate queries to keep that small.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cli">CLI reference</h2>

  <p>
    Every command prints JSON on stdout (<code>--pretty</code> indents it) and takes
    <code>--project &lt;path&gt;</code>, which defaults to the current directory. History, pins, and
    canvases are kept per project. <code>&lt;conn&gt;</code> is a connection id or name.
  </p>

  <DocTable>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Command</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>connection list | new | edit | test | remove</code></td>
        <td>Manage saved connections. <code>new</code> and <code>edit</code> open the browser form and wait for it; <code>new --url</code> prefills it from a URI.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>schema &lt;conn&gt; [--schema S]</code></td>
        <td>Databases, schemas, tables, and columns.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>query &lt;conn&gt; --sql SQL | --file PATH</code></td>
        <td>
          Runs a query (default <code>--limit 1000</code>). <code>--open</code> also puts it in this
          session's console tab, with <code>--note</code> as the comment above it and
          <code>--new-tab</code> for a separate tab. With <code>--open</code>, a write waits for Run
          unless <code>--allow-write</code> is given.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>result &lt;run-id&gt; [--offset N]</code></td>
        <td>Rows of an earlier run, read from its saved result.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>history</code>, <code>pin</code>, <code>unpin</code>, <code>pins</code></td>
        <td>Recent runs and pinned queries, shared with the app.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>canvas new | list | show | add | chart | remove | delete</code></td>
        <td>Build and fix canvases. <code>add --run ID --chart KIND --x COL --y COL</code> adds a query and a chart bound to it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>context</code></td>
        <td>The UI's active tab, its text, and the selection.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>tab list</code>, <code>tab close &lt;id&gt;... | --all-except &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
        <td>The UI's open tabs, and closing them.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>dbt | sqlmesh  models | model | lineage | sync | cli</code></td>
        <td>Models, lineage (<code>--column</code> for column lineage), a connection from the profile or gateway, and logged tool runs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg"><code>ui [--open]</code>, <code>serve</code></td>
        <td>Print or open the UI URL; run the server in the foreground to see why it fails to start.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocTable>

  <p>
    Exit codes: <code>0</code> success, <code>1</code> an API error (printed as an
    <code>error</code> object with a <code>code</code> and a <code>message</code>), <code>2</code> the server could not
    start, <code>3</code> a connection form was cancelled or timed out. <code>dbt cli</code> and
    <code>sqlmesh cli</code> exit with the tool's own code, or <code>127</code> when it is not
    installed. <code>ARRIS_DATA_DIR</code> moves where Arris keeps its data.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
